{
  "summary": "The author, an open source maintainer with dozens of repositories and thousands of stars, laments receiving zero AI-authored pull requests despite colleagues with smaller projects getting multiple AI PRs weekly. After studying high-engagement repositories, they identified practices that correlate with bot activity. Key strategies include writing vague, poorly defined issues that give AI agents interpretive freedom rather than clear problems with reproduction steps; maintaining a CONTRIBUTING.md that explicitly welcomes automated contributions; keeping a large backlog of 200+ open issues to signal unmet needs; and disabling branch protection rules that create friction for automated systems. The author presents these findings with notable irony, noting that while these practices attract AI bots, they would make projects harder for human contributors. The piece humorously frames the pursuit of AI attention as a shift in what open source maintainers value, trading clear communication for bot engagement."
